<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valsad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bulsar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valsad, formerly Bulsar, is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the headquarters of Valsad district. The district covers the coastal plain and the forested hills of the former princely state of Dharampur. The area is well known for its mangoes, especially the Alphonso variety.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valsad.jpg" alt="Valsad" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bhartiya97 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valsad_City_View_from_Parnera_Hill_in_Gujarat,India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Valsad is a town and municipality in the Valsad district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and the district headquarters. It lies north of Vapi and shares borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and the union territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daman">Daman</a> to the south. The old city is about 4 km inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, at an average elevation of 13 metres. The name derives from the Gujarati vad-saal, meaning covered by banyan trees, or a place where banyan and sal trees grow. In the British era it was known as Bulsar. Rainfall is very heavy from June to September under the south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, and little or none falls from October to May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Valsad is known for its historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a> community, and the town was once a hub for Parsis. Udvada, in the district, is described as the primary and oldest Parsi fire temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The family of Farrokh Bulsara, known as Freddie Mercury, originated from Valsad, and so did the family of Field Marshal Sam Manekshaw. The railway station building dates from 1925, and the Callian Baug, an urban park, was given to the municipality in 1929 by Motilal Callianji in memory of his father and opened in 1931. Morarji Desai, later Prime Minister of India, and the lawyer and freedom fighter Bhulabhai Desai are among the notable people of the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave the metropolitan area a population of 170,060. The main language is Gujarati, with Hindi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> and English also spoken, and most people are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> also followed. Valsad lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> main railway line. The district has chemical, textile and paper industries, centred on Vapi, and is also a horticulture region. Nearby sights include Tithal Beach, the Tadkeshwar Mahadev Temple at Abrama, Parnera Hill and Dharampur Palace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valsad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruling family claimed descent from the Sisodias of Mewar, and tradition dates their arrival in the hills inland from <b>Valsad</b> to 1262.</blockquote><a href="/state/dharampur-state">Dharampur State</a></li></ul></section>
